        "old_string": "      \"zoneSize\": \"game-engine contains ~45% of project files but reports cohesion 0.87 and coupling 0.13 — the high file count reflects a genuinely interconnected domain (types, traversal, scoring, biome theming, hooks, rendering components) rather than an over-broad grouping. The zone's internal cohesion exceeds 2x the warning threshold (0.4), confirming it is a natural domain boundary. Route files (play.tsx, worlds.tsx) are overridden out of this zone since they are UI composition layers, not engine logic.\",",
        "new_string": "      \"zoneSize\": \"game-engine originally contained ~45% of project files including app/hooks/ files. The hooks files have been split into a separate react-hooks zone to align zone boundaries with the architecture DAG (engine → hooks). This makes the engine/hooks layer boundary visible to zone-level checks and eliminates the blind spot where zone-boundaries.test.ts could not detect violations that check-layers.sh would catch.\",\n      \"reactHooksSplit\": \"app/hooks/ files are overridden from game-engine into a dedicated react-hooks zone. While the algorithmic community detection correctly identified high cohesion between engine and hooks (they are tightly coupled by design), the architecture DAG treats them as distinct layers: engine is pure game logic, hooks is the React integration layer that bridges engine to components/routes. The zone split ensures that zone-level enforcement (zone-boundaries.test.ts) and directory-level enforcement (check-layers.sh, ESLint no-restricted-paths) share the same boundary model, closing the enforcement blind spot at the engine/hooks seam.\","
      },
